BLI_delete/BLI_copy recursion on Haiku: no NULL path element

Haiku's struct dirent has no d_type, so recursive_operation() stats each entry. It built the
entry's path with BLI_path_join(..., NULL), but since BLI_path_join became a macro that counts
its arguments, the NULL is taken as one more path element and BLI_path_join_array dereferences
it: every recursive delete or copy of a non-empty directory crashed (USDZ export, which deletes
its temporary directory, was the first to do it). The entry's path is already in from_path.

--- a/source/blender/blenlib/intern/fileops.c
+++ b/source/blender/blenlib/intern/fileops.c
@@ -881,10 +881,7 @@
 #  ifdef __HAIKU__
       {
         struct stat st_dir;
-        char filepath[FILE_MAX];
-        BLI_path_join(filepath, sizeof(filepath), startfrom, dirent->d_name, NULL);
-        lstat(filepath, &st_dir);
-        is_dir = S_ISDIR(st_dir.st_mode);
+        is_dir = lstat(from_path, &st_dir) == 0 && S_ISDIR(st_dir.st_mode);
       }
 #  else
       is_dir = (dirent->d_type == DT_DIR);
